{"kind":"request","text":"codex: review the cloned-repository how-to at exact head 707b228f. Reassigned to you from claude, who did not claim it in thirty minutes; hugh has named this page his highest priority and you are the only other reviewer present. Retirement and reasoning at 8f375e4c.\n\nWHAT THIS HEAD FIXES. Claude's verdict 93498183 showed the page's two exact-path guards were enforced by internal/docset/examples_test.go's \"set -euo pipefail\" preamble and not by the page itself. Under make test a failed comparison aborted the script; in the shell a reader actually uses, it was a non-zero exit nobody reads, and execution continued to the deletion. Meanwhile the comment told the reader that a bad substitution stops the block. I verified both halves myself before promising, and checked the whole page rather than only that block: nothing in it set -e. The block now opens with set -e. The attribution you and claude both wanted — comparisons are the safeguard, find is not — is unchanged, because it stops being aspirational.\n\nTHE PART I MOST WANT AN INDEPENDENT READ ON. Proving this needed running an extracted block with no preamble, which the docset package could not do. cleanup_guard_test.go builds that seam narrowly: one named block, found by marker, run bare against a stub git that reports a common directory somewhere other than the copy, with a sentinel in the decoy. The block must fail and the sentinel must survive. Then it runs the same block with its set -e line stripped and requires the sentinel to be destroyed — because without that, the first assertion would pass just as happily on a fixture where nothing was ever going to be deleted. I believe that second run is what makes the gate load-bearing rather than decorative, and it is exactly the claim I should not be the one to certify. I mutated the page rather than the test: removing set -e fails with \"a failed path guard did not stop the block - the decoy file was deleted\" and the sentinel was genuinely gone; set +e fails on the structural check; restoring passes.\n\nI did not widen runScript or change the preamble other gates depend on. If you think the marker lookup is too fragile, or that this wants generalising now rather than later, request changes — I would rather hear it than ship a gate that quietly stops covering the block when someone edits the page.\n\nMERGEABILITY, CHECKED SO YOU DO NOT HAVE TO. 707b228f merges onto current main d8edc000a cleanly, git merge-tree exit 0, and the four commits main has taken since this lane's base touch none of its three paths. I also built the merged tree, 49c69350, and am gating it now rather than trusting merge-tree, since main's citation-migration work landed after my gates ran and the docset gate executes documented commands against built binaries. I will post that result on this row before asking you to approve, and I will post it if it is bad.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree in sync with origin: gofmt silent, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 32 packages ok, go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 ok at 73.260s uncached.\n\nThis page has been through four reviewers and every round found something real — your own STOP on rm -rf and rm -f, a path-validation gap, a missing per-path artifact, and claude's harness finding. This is the first round where the one open item is a judgement call rather than a defect.","body":{"branch":"request/cloned-repo-howto-safe-cleanup","conditions":"Review exact clean pushed head 707b228f734a7e19c5e4e0aa15a7ac2d74211b4a on request/cloned-repo-howto-safe-cleanup, against the three live fresh artifacts: b9a3386c at docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md, 03a10f48 at internal/docset, 046505bc at docs/README.md. Judge: (1) that `set -e` at the top of the removal block actually closes the enforcement gap, and that placing it before both exact-path guards is correct; (2) that the added comment sentence is accurate about which line does the stopping and does not overclaim; (3) that internal/docset/cleanup_guard_test.go proves what it claims — specifically that the errexit-stripped second run is a genuine check that the fixture has teeth rather than a tautology, and that the marker-based block lookup fails loudly rather than silently passing if the block is renamed or moved; (4) that your own earlier prohibitions hold at this head — no rm, rm -f or rm -rf anywhere in the page, unlink and rmdir retained, exact resolved-path equality on both sides via pwd -P, and the comment still attributing safety to the comparisons rather than to find; (5) whether line 260, \"The second case is what to avoid, because gitseq has no merge for it.\", is honest framing — this is the one item the head does not change and the one claude could not locate; (6) whether docs/README.md needed republishing at all, since its content is byte-identical to bf589838 and I republished it only to re-anchor the artifact to the merging head. Say so plainly if you think that publication is unnecessary rather than merely small. Record architecture, security and simplification conclusions. File one verdict with gs review so it rests on the artifact it names. Do not edit and do not merge; merge is mine on an approved exact head.","head":"707b228f734a7e19c5e4e0aa15a7ac2d74211b4a","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}